{"key": "background",
    "value": "Lena Maris Kovacs, born 14 March 1981 in Szeged, Hungary, is a Hungarian historian specializing in social and cultural history of small Central European towns in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. She is known for her work with ego-documents like diaries, letters, and local newspapers, and she integrates visual materials such as postcards and photographs into her research."
  }

{"key": "education",
    "value": "Studied history at University of Szeged (BA), then Eötvös Loránd University (MA) focusing on social history and microhistory. Completed PhD at Central European University (2013) on diaries from Austro-Hungarian borderlands (1880–1918), analyzing language, identity, and daily life across ethnic groups."
  }

{"key": "research focus",
    "value": "Studies everyday life in small towns through personal writings and administrative records. Emphasizes non-elite experiences, multilingual sources, and the interplay between written and visual materials. Uses digital tools like databases, spreadsheets, and GIS to analyze patterns in archival data."
  }

{"key": "publications",
    "value": "Published two major monographs: 'Streets of Memory' (2017) on small-town diaries in the Habsburg Monarchy; 'Markets, Fairs, and Frontiers' (2021) on economic life in borderland towns (1870–1930). Also contributes methodological articles and co-edits volumes on historical cartography and local associations."
  }

{"key": "background",
    "value": "Elena Maris, born 14 October 1983 in Lyrisport, a coastal town with humid climate and maritime trade. Grew up near warehouses with old nautical maps, flags, and ship parts. Volunteered at local museum during school, joined drawing club, took maritime history course."
  }

{"key": "education",
    "value": "Studied art history and chemistry at University of North Estara (graduated 2005). Interned in campus conservation lab. Later earned Master of Conservation from Institute for Cultural Materials (2009). Thesis on aging of synthetic varnishes on acrylic paintings (1960–1980)."
  }

{"key": "career",
    "value": "Started as junior conservator at Central Museum of Visual Cultures (Arden). Promoted to senior paintings conservator in 2013. Led team, introduced barcoded condition-tracking system, coordinated loan procedures. Expanded to mixed-media and installation artworks from 2016, focusing on artist intent, aging, and technical integration."
  }

{"key": "ethics & methods",
    "value": "Follows ethical guidelines: respect for original materials, reversibility, transparency. Uses scientific methods, detailed documentation (photos, cross-sections, UV/IR imaging), and consults standards. Emphasizes minimal intervention, material research, and record-keeping."
  }

{"key": "person",
